{
 "batch": "item-briefs-2026-11-01-reg-backfill-1b",
 "generated_at": "2026-11-01T14:59:47.940Z",
 "authored_by": "session-lane",
 "entries": [
  {
   "item_id": "0b6537ea-1c85-41b9-81ed-1486fd72ea18",
   "source_pool_hash": "589135963e96319b56f2e6d4a7532401c6f668029fcb2c846f4a9e630945521b",
   "metadata": {
    "why_matters": {
     "value": "Ships that serve offshore installations, not only ships carrying cargo or passengers, come within the EU greenhouse gas emissions monitoring rules from 1 January 2025. A company that operates such a ship on voyages to and from Member State ports therefore takes on monitoring and reporting duties that it did not carry before.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "0fc74339-d5c1-445a-a83b-6b94c583bc55",
      "text": "amended Regulation (EU) 2015/757, notably to provide for the inclusion of greenhouse gas emissions from offshore ships within the scope of that Regulation from 1 January 2025."
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1b",
    "judges": {
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Inclusion of offshore-ship emissions \"from 1 January 2025\" is in the quote; the monitoring and reporting duties follow from held sections (Part AA coverage of voyages to/from Member State ports, verified monitoring plan within three months of first port call). No price pass-through asserted."
     },
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Date is the quoted one. Held sections state ships \"designed or certified to perform service activities offshore or at offshore installations\" and voyages to/from Member State ports of call, matching the value. Note: held sections give a 5,000 GT line only for ETS, not for monitoring, so the value's silence on tonnage is not a widening the item can show."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": "10605225-088d-451a-a3a4-84108ad2d6f6",
   "source_pool_hash": "df34fbdbe7d335d32ea91019f436832cc9fcec266316814edb22f5798b460364",
   "metadata": {
    "what_it_changes": {
     "value": "Applies from 1 January 2020 as the EU electricity market rulebook; its duties run to Member States, regulators and transmission system operators, not to freight companies.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "5bfa6ab0-da7e-4963-b80f-0557bdcf4cfc",
      "text": "It shall apply from 1 January 2020."
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1b",
    "judges": {
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "No consequence chain. Date is the quote (Article 71(2)); the addressee statement rests on held sections (\"Every 'shall' duty identified ... runs to a Member State, a national regulatory authority, a transmission system operator, or an electricity undertaking\")."
     },
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Date and addressees are within quote plus held sections. Caveat for the author: the next sentence in the same paragraph (Article 71(2)) says Articles 14, 15, 22(4), 23(3) and (6), 35, 36 and 62 apply from entry into force; the value states 1 January 2020 flat and the held sections do not mention the exception. Passes at sentence level; flagged."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": "128b6a2e-cf78-4c9f-b03d-9256a3df5222",
   "source_pool_hash": "613f4292788b7865f3aa5923f07a883a966c12169e0d3b7efb59f5c61b41ade9",
   "metadata": {
    "what_it_changes": {
     "value": "The updated rules for monitoring and reporting greenhouse gas emissions under the EU emissions trading rules apply from 1 January 2024; some points apply from 1 July 2024.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "65a950ee-f201-4433-97e7-4577be1be876",
      "text": "It shall apply from 1 January 2024. However, Article 1, points (24), (25)(a)(iii), (25)(d), (27), (30)(a), (31), (33), (34)(d) and (35) shall apply from 1 July 2024."
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1b",
    "judges": {
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Dates are the quoted application dates; no consequence chain asserted."
     },
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Quote is the application clause of Implementing Regulation 2023/2122 amending 2018/2066 (monitoring and reporting under Directive 2003/87/EC): 1 January 2024 generally, listed points of Article 1 from 1 July 2024. Value claims no more."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": "1cda60cd-971a-4294-b90f-fdef2d7ef6da",
   "source_pool_hash": "2a49586dbfc0c2513c7009c25618b0d13bacab78042993cdb94251b7041272a0",
   "metadata": {
    "why_matters": {
     "value": "Airlines flying within Brazil must cut emissions by using sustainable aviation fuel, with the first minimum cut due in 2027. The duty falls on the airlines, not on forwarders. A forwarder booking Brazilian domestic air legs should check whether its fuel surcharge clauses cover that fuel cost and ask carriers what compliance data they will share.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "fe9d3fd3-aac3-4815-b778-199a4b6c36a6",
      "text": "Os operadores aéreos ficam obrigados a reduzir as emissões de GEE em suas operações domésticas por meio do uso de SAF, conforme os seguintes percentuais mínimos de redução: I – 1% (um por cento), a partir de 1º de janeiro de 2027;"
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1b",
    "judges": {
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "The consequence is a check, not a pass-through claim: the duty on airlines and the 2027 start are in the quote; the advice to check fuel surcharge clauses and ask carriers about compliance data rests on held section 11 (labelled analytical inference) and section 3. No surcharge is asserted to follow without a span or label."
     },
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Population, class and date match: 'operadores aéreos' in domestic operations, first minimum cut from 1 January 2027. 'Not on forwarders' follows from the defined obligated party (held section 7, Art. 2 XVI, aircraft operators); note held section 7 still marks a forwarder's exclusion for counsel confirmation, so the wording is firm but no wider than the definition."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": "20acdaeb-df99-45b9-8254-28ba20a292ea",
   "source_pool_hash": "d962f417cc13cc8dfdc72ade9d584798cbe7ea6baafcc80c297951267ae20e64",
   "metadata": {
    "what_it_changes": {
     "value": "For plans submitted after 1 March 2023 that need the extra funding, a REPowerEU chapter with measures, milestones and targets is required; the duty sits with Member States.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "8ac9bdf2-3eb4-4e81-8101-fc0bec3ec8cf",
      "text": "Recovery and resilience plans submitted to the Commission after 1 March 2023 that require the use of additional funding under Articles 14, 21a or 21b, shall include a REPowerEU chapter containing measures and their corresponding milestones and targets."
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1b",
    "judges": {
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Requirement and trigger date are the quoted sentence (Article 21c(1)); no downstream cost or funding effect asserted. \"Duty sits with Member States\" rests on held sections (a Member State's REPowerEU chapter)."
     },
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Same population (plans submitted after 1 March 2023), same condition (require additional funding under Articles 14, 21a or 21b), same content (measures, milestones, targets)."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": "3e756291-f79e-487e-84d5-efc535cfef27",
   "source_pool_hash": "f4b847bfedaba4bf8e2a0deacdc75de7dfe56202a63ab0c7e6097fffe3808d97",
   "metadata": {
    "what_it_changes": {
     "value": "China's carbon market is set to cover all major industrial sectors by 2027, with paid allowances beside free ones by 2030, so more Chinese industries will face a direct carbon cost.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "a6136e56-e3a0-47a9-9050-ebe1e5f64446",
      "text": "by 2027, the country's carbon trading market will encompass all major industrial sectors, and the national voluntary greenhouse gas emission reduction trading market will expand to include all key fields. By 2030, China will have essentially established a national carbon trading market based on a cap-and-trade system with both free and paid allocations."
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1b",
    "judges": {
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Coverage of all major industrial sectors by 2027 and a free-plus-paid allocation by 2030 are in the quote; that covered entities carry the cost is stated in held section 4 (covered entities surrender allowances) and section 2 (carbon costs of covered counterparties). No freight price or surcharge pass-through is asserted."
     },
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Same population (China's national carbon market), same dates (2027, 2030) and same scheme as the quote ('will encompass all major industrial sectors', 'free and paid allocations'). 'Set to' keeps the forward, guideline-level status held section 1 states. Nothing wider than the instrument."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": "40c05a1e-f834-4a14-b2dd-a16707df703c",
   "source_pool_hash": "2fe5ce38d5bfdfedf10708ba43640b7541f8be2b38f0af6af11a89a9d0896222",
   "metadata": {
    "what_it_changes": {
     "value": "If adopted, zero-emission trucks may carry extra weight because the technology adds vehicle weight; the text is still under negotiation, so no start date is set.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "bfeb7563-551d-4896-be4e-ed0ff43f82a9",
      "text": "It provides for new incentives for using low-emission lorries by allowing additional weight for vehicles using zero-emission technologies, as they tend to increase a vehicle's weight."
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1b",
    "judges": {
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "The reason (technology adds vehicle weight) is the quote's own clause; 'still under negotiation' rests on held section 6 (trilogue started 9 December 2025) and section 1 (proposal, not enacted); 'no start date is set' rests on held section 6 (no conclusion date stated) and the section 14 milestones, none of which fixes an application date. No price consequence asserted."
     },
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Population and status match: zero-emission lorries/trucks and an 'if adopted' proposal; the value adds no figure, region or duty. The 4-tonne figure and axle detail in the held sections are not claimed."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": "5561231f-3e3d-4e6a-90a2-1f3f4baf2f1b",
   "source_pool_hash": "3fc8e83a1854d5c5164e077bbf199b9ce457dcf6f27a84d148b3201b34f20a0b",
   "metadata": {
    "why_matters": {
     "value": "Heavy-duty vehicles in service are sampled and tested to check that the emission and fuel consumption values recorded for them match how the vehicles really perform. A freight business that runs heavy-duty trucks or relies on their declared figures is therefore dealing with values that are independently checked, while this Regulation puts its duties on type-approval authorities and manufacturers, not on carriers.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "27a26b93-13ab-4eea-9b03-92ffe512815a",
      "text": "to verify for those manufacturers, on the basis of appropriate and representative vehicle samples, that the CO 2 emission and fuel consumption values recorded in the customer information files correspond to the CO 2 emissions from, and fuel consumption of, heavy-duty vehicles in-service"
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1b",
    "judges": {
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Verification of recorded CO2 and fuel consumption values against in-service performance on vehicle samples is the quote; the addressee statement (type-approval authorities and manufacturers) rests on held sections. No cost or price effect asserted."
     },
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Limited to recorded values for heavy-duty vehicles in service, sampled. Caveat: held sections state granting authorities may skip manufacturers under 100 vehicles, so \"independently checked\" is general but not claimed for every manufacturer; passes."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": "56031fd3-d7ae-45e5-a1d0-bde3e3c30383",
   "source_pool_hash": "1cd4523c3b1bf2e4276c78b53e4aeae34d982b391114fc2aabe1fecd2e898308",
   "metadata": {
    "what_it_changes": {
     "value": "Within the EEA, airlines get 25% fewer free allowances in 2024, 50% fewer in 2025 and buy all from 2026, raising intra-EEA air rates; international flights to or from the EEA fall under CORSIA.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "7d5cc990-836d-4eb6-989d-f3feabc79864",
      "text": "Additionally, the revision appropriately implements ICAO's CORSIA through the EU ETS Directive. This will be applied to international flights departing from or arriving at airports within the European Economic Area (EEA). For flights within the EEA, only the EU ETS will continue to apply. The revision introduces the following changes: Full auctioning from 2026 Free allocation to aircraft operators will be reduced by 25% in 2024 and by 50% 2025, moving to full auctioning for the sector by 2026."
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1b",
    "judges": {
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Allowance cuts and full auctioning from 2026 are in the quote; the rise in intra-EEA air rates rests on held sections 4, 8 and 3 (airlines recover ETS cost through rates and surcharges on intra-EEA lanes). No unsourced surcharge figure."
     },
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Every figure and year (25%, 2024, 50%, 2025, 2026) is in the verbatim quote. Scope now matches: \"within the EEA\" for the allowance cuts and rate effect, and CORSIA limited to international flights to or from the EEA, as the quote states. Nothing wider than the instrument."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": "68af8b45-fbbf-4ba1-add8-2c1761d2d120",
   "source_pool_hash": "6a6f3eccff1d6396e9cacdd8b55c498dad2c9b7fc4da927348883fafe4ab1037",
   "metadata": {
    "why_matters": {
     "value": "This Regulation applies to shipments of waste between Member States, into the Union, out of the Union, and in transit through it, though it names some exclusions. A carrier moving a covered load shares the duty to keep the shipment documents available in the vehicle when they cannot be shown online.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "4a6fccb8-151f-4bb9-ae27-a23bac7f13a0",
      "text": "This Regulation applies to: (a) shipments of waste between Member States, with or without transit through third countries; (b) shipments of waste imported into the Union from third countries; (c) shipments of waste exported from the Union to third countries; (d) shipments of waste in transit through the Union on the way to or from third countries."
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1b",
    "judges": {
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "No price or cost pass-through. The carrier duty is stated by held claim/section (Art 16(4): \"Where the electronic documents cannot be made available online during transport, the notifier and carrier shall ensure that the documents are available by other means in the transport vehicle\"), same condition as the value."
     },
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Scope sentence restates the quote (a)-(d) verbatim in substance and says \"though it names some exclusions\", matching the held Exclusions from Scope section (Art 2(2)-(3)); \"Any\" is gone. Carrier duty limited to the offline-documents case, as held Art 16(4) states."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": "6cdc920f-6110-412a-b4f8-7b6c7fabdda5",
   "source_pool_hash": "72c5361d2a8191ac648f82733e1073bf93b7a63a155619b4536dc08603633b06",
   "metadata": {
    "why_matters": {
     "value": "This Directive moves back the dates from which Member States apply certain corporate sustainability reporting and due diligence requirements. For the first set of companies in scope of the due diligence directive the date is postponed by one year to give them more time to prepare, so a freight business in scope gets extra preparation time, but the requirements are postponed and not removed.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "18c3a8fd-6432-49a2-ab93-56357093d2fe",
      "text": "The date from which Member States are to apply Directive (EU) 2024/1760 should be postponed by one year for the first set of companies that fall within the scope of that Directive in order to give companies more time to prepare for the requirements of that Directive"
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1b",
    "judges": {
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Postponement by one year for the first set of companies and its purpose (\"to give companies more time to prepare\") are the quote. \"Postponed and not removed\" rests on held sections (substantive CSRD obligations \"remain in force from the postponed dates\"; Wave 1 CSDDD application 26 July 2028). No price pass-through."
     },
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Scope is the first set of companies in scope of Directive 2024/1760, one year, as quoted; the opening sentence restates the Directive's own title. Note the quote is a recital (\"should be postponed\"); the held sections state the operative dates."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": "9ca79a35-b51a-442d-8220-7ae20b6086a4",
   "source_pool_hash": "855e70285e3093530ebf131a71b5f4307a3b3065677c97227f0d0c9527a943fe",
   "metadata": {
    "why_matters": {
     "value": "When a customs risk management system flags a product entering or leaving the Union as one that might fall under a decision communicated under this Regulation, customs must suspend its release for free circulation or export. For a shipment you handle, that means the cargo can be held at that point.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "0a45ce0a-2a62-4b37-bdc8-1fb05e8ec83a",
      "text": "Where customs authorities identify, through their relevant risk management system, that a product entering or leaving the Union market might, according to a decision communicated pursuant to Article 26(3), be in violation of Article 3, they shall suspend the release of that product for free circulation or export."
     }
    },
    "what_it_changes": {
     "value": "The ban on placing products made with forced labour on the EU market, or exporting them, applies from 14 December 2027; a few articles applied earlier, from 13 December 2024.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "0a45ce0a-2a62-4b37-bdc8-1fb05e8ec83a",
      "text": "It shall apply from 14 December 2027. However, Articles 5(3), 7, 8, 9(2), 11, 33, 35 and 37(3) shall apply from 13 December 2024."
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1b",
    "judges": {
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Suspension of release is stated by the quote; \"the cargo can be held at that point\" restates it; no price or cost pass-through. what_it_changes dates are in its quote."
     },
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Trigger now matches the whole Article 28 sentence: risk-management flag, product \"might\" fall under a decision under Art 26(3), customs suspend release. what_it_changes: 14 Dec 2027 general, listed articles 13 Dec 2024, as quoted; the ban reading rests on held Art 3."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": "ac349a70-606d-4eb3-ac19-5a4a0facd07c",
   "source_pool_hash": "097353e9f79c19a01c78c347d64165b29a2035fedf87311b6a83446898d787c2",
   "metadata": {
    "why_matters": {
     "value": "The battery duties fall on producers, and that includes any business that first supplies batteries on a professional basis from another country, such as an importer. A forwarder is caught only if its own transactions fit that wording, which still needs legal review. Until then, check whether any battery shipment you handle is bought and resupplied in the EU in your own name.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "92553b72-5c30-4c26-b39e-ebeb94d7b270",
      "text": "is established in a Member State and supplies for the first time in that Member State on a professional basis, batteries, including those incorporated in appliances, light means of transport or other vehicles, from another Member State or from a third country"
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1b",
    "judges": {
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "No price or cost consequence. The duty on producers and the first-supply limb are in the quote and held sections 7 and 10; the forwarder-caught-only-if-wording-fits, legal review step rests on held section 7 (Legal Confirmation Required on importation for resupply as 'supplying for the first time')."
     },
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Value tracks the quote's limb (first professional supply from another Member State or a third country) and the held 'importation for resupply' and 'own name' wording, and hedges the forwarder's position for legal review. One drop: the quote's 'established in a Member State' is not repeated in 'any business', but the value confines the check to EU resupply; judged within the quote's scope."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": "bf54dc59-ad47-4e2e-9984-cc4cd0206435",
   "source_pool_hash": "3805086f01b987eab311d66934aad5e6cf8bad3361c4c96c08f00eda84dc7e53",
   "metadata": {
    "what_it_changes": {
     "value": "The EU's 55% cut by 2030 is met sector by sector: aviation fuel suppliers and aircraft operators, ship operators and truck manufacturers each face new rules, phasing in this decade.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "402851ec-b867-4a74-ad08-58a86458ba9d",
      "text": "a reduction of net greenhouse gas emissions by at least 55 % compared to 1990 by 2030. In order to achieve this, all economic sectors, including the transport sector, have to take rapid steps to decarbonise."
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1b",
    "judges": {
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "The sector-by-sector step rests on the quote (55% by 2030; transport must decarbonise) and held sections 2, 4, 8 and 14 (ReFuelEU on fuel suppliers, ETS and FuelEU on shipping companies, HDV CO2 standards on manufacturers, 2024-2030 dates). No price or surcharge pass-through asserted."
     },
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Each party is named as the held sections name it: fuel suppliers (ReFuelEU, sec 4/8) and aircraft operators (ETS, sec 4), \"ship operators\" = the shipping company that sec 4 and 8 call the regulated entity under ETS maritime and FuelEU Maritime, truck manufacturers (HDV CO2 standards, sec 4/8b). The road-carrier overstatement is gone; \"this decade\" matches the 2024-2030 timeline."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": "c509a0cd-263d-48fc-8d0b-160f786bdbb0",
   "source_pool_hash": "448b28983b654cbc6e6506b08e49a0ce3864714eba3aa62f54bbde87c4f55159",
   "metadata": {
    "why_matters": {
     "value": "A customs broker or forwarder that lodges import declarations as an indirect customs representative must itself hold authorised CBAM declarant status before importing, and acts as the declarant for the importer that appointed it. That puts certificate purchase and reporting duties on the forwarder's desk, not only the client's. Check now whether you are acting in that role for any importer of CBAM goods.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "edbcbb19-a274-4f02-8bb0-d8b807781181",
      "text": "An indirect customs representative shall obtain the status of authorised CBAM declarant prior to importing goods into the customs territory of the Union. An indirect customs representative shall act as an authorised CBAM declarant where the indirect customs representative is appointed by an importer in accordance with Article 18 of Regulation (EU) No 952/2013"
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1b",
    "judges": {
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Obligation to hold authorised declarant status before importing, and to act as declarant where appointed, is in the quote. 'Certificate purchase and reporting duties' rests on held sections: section 2 (an ICR with that status 'would be subject to the full obligations of that role, including CBAM declaration submission and certificate surrender'), section 12 (obligations are 'declaration, certificate purchase, certificate surrender'), sections 8 and 3 (declaration and certificates held by the authorised declarant, 2027 purchase window). No pass-through to price asserted."
     },
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "The author's flag is not borne out: the quote makes the ICR an authorised declarant, and the held sections state what a declarant owes (declaration, purchase, surrender), so no duty is invented beyond them. Value is confined to an indirect customs representative lodging declarations. Reservation: the quote stops before 'and agrees to act as authorised CBAM declarant' (held section 2 has it), so 'acts as the declarant' leaves out that condition; it matches the quote as given."
     }
    }
   }
  }
 ]
}
